   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cecd69db",
   "source": "## Q1. How do we decide whether to transport the floating-point and vector registers?\n\n**Answer:** The QEMU plugin decides at drain time by *asking QEMU which registers it advertises*. If QEMU exposes the FP registers, we set `HYBRID_FLAG_FP` and carry them. If it exposes the vector registers with a sane `vlenb`, we set `HYBRID_FLAG_V` and carry them. It is capability detection, not a hand-set switch. **Both are supported today.**\n\n### One idea behind both: a handle means \"this CPU has it\"\n\n`resolve_handles()` looks up every register by name. `find_reg` returns a handle if QEMU advertised that register, else `NULL`:\n\n```c\n// qemu_plugin/hybrid_handoff.c:121\nstatic struct qemu_plugin_register *find_reg(GArray *regs, const char *name) {\n    for (guint i = 0; i < regs->len; i++) {\n        // ... if the name matches, return rd->handle;\n    }\n    return NULL;   // not advertised -> this CPU does not expose it\n}\n```\n\nSo \"handle is non-NULL\" is the plugin's proxy for \"this CPU has this register.\" FP and V both build on this one check.\n\n### Floating-point: any FP handle -> transport\n\n```c\n// hybrid_handoff.c:296\nint has_fp = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < 32; i++) {\n    s.fpr[i] = read_reg64(v->fpr[i]);\n    if (v->fpr[i]) has_fp = 1;      // at least one f-register was advertised\n}\nif (has_fp) s.flags |= HYBRID_FLAG_FP;\ns.fcsr = read_reg64(v->fcsr);        // fcsr rides the FP flag\n```\n\nRule: **carry FP iff QEMU advertised the F/D extension** (it exposes `f0..f31`). `fcsr` travels with the same flag.\n\n### Vector: a small policy, not just a bool\n\nV has more failure modes than FP: V can be *disabled at runtime*, the stub can *half-advertise* V, or the hardware `VLEN` can be *wider than the wire format*. A pure decision function covers every case:\n\n```c\n// include/hybrid/v_drain_policy.h  (its own truth table)\n//   no vlenb, no other V          -> SKIP    (no V at all)\n//   no vlenb, but vtype/vl/...     -> ABORT   (partial advertisement -> refuse)\n//   vlenb advertised, no other V   -> SKIP\n//   vlenb, other V, vlenb == 0     -> SKIP    (V disabled at runtime)\n//   vlenb, other V, vlenb > 128    -> ABORT   (L11: wire format cannot hold it)\n//   otherwise                      -> OK\n```\n\nThe plugin calls it and acts on the verdict:\n\n```c\n// hybrid_handoff.c:379\nHybridVDrainPolicy vpol = hybrid_v_drain_policy(v->vlenb != NULL, has_other_v, s.vlenb);\nif (vpol == HYBRID_V_DRAIN_ABORT) { /* print reason */ exit(2); }\nif (vpol == HYBRID_V_DRAIN_OK) {\n    s.flags |= HYBRID_FLAG_V;\n    for (int i = 0; i < 32; i++)                    // copy vlenb bytes per v-register\n        read_reg_bytes(v->vreg[i], &s.vreg[i * 128], s.vlenb);\n}\n```\n\nRule: **carry V iff QEMU advertised the V extension with a non-zero `vlenb` <= 128 bytes** (VLEN <= 1024). A partial or oversized advertisement is a hard error (`exit(2)`), never a silent skip -- silently skipping would make the resume side mis-restore.\n\n### The consumer obeys the same flags\n\nThe flags are the single gate on the far side. `--verify` compares FP / V blocks only when the matching flag is set:\n\n```cpp\n// driver/state_diff.cpp:50\nif (ref.flags & HYBRID_FLAG_FP) { /* compare f0..f31, fcsr */ }\nif (ref.flags & HYBRID_FLAG_V)  { /* compare vstart/vtype/vl/vlenb/vxsat/vxrm/vcsr, v0..v31 */ }\n```\n\n`ResumeDriver` (vsim side) likewise restores each block only under its flag: `fcsr` under FP, and the full V restore order under V (see CLAUDE.md, \"V-State Transport\").\n\n### Supported? Yes.\n\n| Block | Decided by | Flag | Carried | Verified |\n|-------|-----------|------|---------|----------|\n| `f0..f31` + `fcsr` | any f-register handle advertised | `HYBRID_FLAG_FP` | yes | yes (`state_diff.cpp:50`) |\n| `v0..v31` + V-CSRs | `hybrid_v_drain_policy() == OK` | `HYBRID_FLAG_V` | yes (via scratch page) | yes (`state_diff.cpp:56`) |\n\nFixtures that exercise them: `rt_all_fprs.S` (FP), `rt_c_v_regs` and `rt_c_v_csrs` (V).",
